A||||7||fiero56||"graphoil" seal||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-12-1999||07:35 PM||||I finished installing my exhaust today, went pretty smoothly.  But, after I installed it, I looked at my Haynes manual(I know I did it backwards). Haynes says to replace the graphoil seal if you take apart the exhaust. The seal is on the larger fitting on the y-pipe. At the end where exhaust exits going to the cat. Why is it supposed to be replaced, and will it leak since I didn't replace it?||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-12-1999||08:50 PM||||If you meen the seal between the y and the catalyst pipe. yes you should replace it. if or how soon it may leak&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt;&gt; who knows.<P>That seal is designed to wear. it take the pounding of the engine movment away from the rest of the system.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-12-1999||11:22 PM||||Hey Ogre,<BR>Your back, we were just talking about you <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.<BR>Is this a normal stock item for GM? I've never seen one of these on the Fiero Store catalog. I'll try to replace it pretty soon, along with my plenum gaskets.<BR>Thanks||||reg||7
Z||000003||DJRice||12-13-1999||02:44 PM||||Im thinking those are run-of-the-mill exhaust parts. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||12-13-1999||06:56 PM||||I hope they are.  I fired her up for a second today, sounded like the lawnmower. I have a leak somewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||12-13-1999||07:41 PM||||If the seal you are talking about is the kind that looks like it is some kind of compressed steel wire with a coating over it that goes in where manifolds meet tail pipes, I have fixed a leaky one by coating it with red silicone and letting it dry before re-installing it. It is still leak free after 4 months of driving.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||12-13-1999||08:27 PM||||The seal is stock at nearly any parts shop. (((NEVER NEVER EVER shop Fiero Store FIRST! Always check local shops first. Fiero uses MANY standard GM parts.)))<P>The seals GM uses for this tend to be a graphite compound on wire mesh reinforcement. They have a greasy feel to them. Don't use the hard ceramic or the white ones. ((I don't even think you can get the white ones anymore. they were asbestose)||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-13-1999||10:48 PM||||When I pulled it out I figured it was made of asbestos.  Then I read graphoil, and Haynes had no warnings about it so I figured it must have been made out of a different material. Pretty weird stuff they come up with. I hope it's leaking at the bottom of the y-pipe, it's the easiest to work on.||||reg||1
